                "13289c9414b6838eaa8544cbfc393b5f2ef9672b"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/59/80259/1",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "PeterYinGit",
                "email": "peter.yin@quantatw.com",
                "username": "PeterYinGit"
            },
            "createdOn": 1747129193,
            "author": {
                "name": "PeterYinGit",
                "email": "peter.yin@quantatw.com",
                "username": "PeterYinGit"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why would an external user care that a button was pressed?  This doesn't seem log-worthy."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "PeterYinGit",
                        "email": "peter.yin@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "PeterYinGit"
                    },
                    "message": "The button event serves as a reminder that it was triggered manually by the user, \nnot due to an unexpected reset, DC, or AC cycle. It is mainly used for debugging purposes"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm not against this, our field support staff have been hinting to us that they want something like this as well.\n\nHowever, since a press is always followed by a release, are events for both necessary?  What if we just had an event for button released with the press time in it, which is definitely known to the button code.  (assuming you're using phosphor-buttons)"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "PeterYinGit",
                        "email": "peter.yin@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "PeterYinGit"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, this is a good point. If there are no other concerns, we can remove the pressed/released logs and only record ButtonStateChanged."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "PeterYinGit",
                        "email": "peter.yin@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "PeterYinGit"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "What I was suggesting was to not generate 2 events for every button press and just do one on the release, including the button press time if that was useful.\n\nThough I suppose even if both types of events are defined that doesn't mean they have to be used by the code generating the event."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "PeterYinGit",
                        "email": "peter.yin@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "PeterYinGit"
                    },
                    "message": "Could you be more specific about the event format? Should we define the pass/release events and their timeline in a log format?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "Right here https://github.com/openbmc/phosphor-buttons/blob/master/src/power_button.cpp#L86  is where the code emits a signal saying a button was released, with the press time in the payload.  I was thinking the event could mirror that.  Just a single event with the press time in it."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Chassis/Buttons/Button.events.yaml",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Don't we have an enum for this?  Rather than a free-form string?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Chassis/Buttons/Button.events.yaml",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "PeterYinGit",
                        "email": "peter.yin@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "PeterYinGit"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
